From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a nested desktop takes the host's shortcuts

A desktop running as a window inside an existing Wayland session had no
Meta key. Every binding the shell claims is a Meta chord --
`packages/shell-manganese/src/keyboard/bindings.ts` is all of them -- and
a host compositor matches its own bindings BEFORE it sends a key to the
focused client, so under sway Mod4+Return opened sway's terminal and this
engine was never told a key had been pressed at all. `grabShortcut` goes
no further than this browser process, so there was nothing there for it
to compete with. Nothing failed and nothing logged: the shell simply
looked broken, and every Meta binding in it was untestable in the one
configuration a developer actually runs.

`zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibit_unstable_v1` is the protocol for saying
"not while this surface has the keyboard". `WaylandConnection` already
binds `zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibit_manager_v1`, and
`WaylandKeyboard::CreateShortcutsInhibitor` is upstream's own request
against it -- so this drives that rather than issuing `inhibit_shortcuts`
a second time from a new object of our own.

WHAT UPSTREAM HAS NO PATH TO IS THE REASON. `CreateShortcutsInhibitor`'s
only caller is `WaylandKeyboard::CreateKeyboardHook`, reached from
`OzonePlatformWayland::CreateKeyboardHook` -- the Keyboard Lock API, a
page calling `navigator.keyboard.lock()` on a window it asked to make
fullscreen. A nested desktop is deliberately NOT fullscreen
(`--start-fullscreen` is the drm platform's; `spawn.rs` says why), and
putting the lock call in the shell would make every shell responsible for
a property of a window it does not own. The desktop is not asking to lock
keys for a page, it is saying that it is the desktop.
`WaylandSurface::SetKeyboardShortcutsInhibition(bool)` reads like the
seam for this and is not one: at this pin it is declared in
`wayland_surface.h` and never defined or called anywhere in the tree.

A SWITCH RATHER THAN THE PLATFORM NAME. Every nested run of this engine
reaches this code and most of them are not desktops: each
`packages/domicile-engine/scripts/guard-*.sh` that runs under
`under-wayland.sh` starts `chrome --ozone-platform=wayland` by hand, and
a guard that silently swallows the keymap of the session it is running in
is one nobody can debug next to. Taking a person's shortcuts away is a
decision about the run rather than a property of the platform, so
`domicile-launch` makes it: `packages/domicile-launch/src/spawn.rs`
passes `--domicile-inhibit-host-shortcuts` on the wayland platform and
nowhere else, beside `--start-fullscreen` and for the same reason.

THE INHIBITOR LIVES EXACTLY AS LONG AS THE WINDOW IS MAPPED. Created in
`SetUpShellIntegration()` beside the appmenu and reset in `Hide()` beside
it, because `Show()` builds a fresh `xdg_toplevel` and runs
`SetUpShellIntegration()` again -- and a second inhibitor for a surface
and seat that already have one is a protocol error the compositor answers
by dropping the connection. `wl::Object`'s deleter is what hands the
shortcuts back.

THE USER'S ESCAPE HATCH IS THE COMPOSITOR'S, and it has to be. The
inhibitor is honored per seat and only while the surface holds keyboard
focus, so clicking away restores everything; while the desktop IS
focused, `bindsym --inhibited` is what keeps a sway binding alive through
it. `docs/RUNNING-A-DESKTOP.md` says so, because a keymap that goes dead
the moment the desktop takes focus needs a sentence a user can find.

NOT MEASURED, AND NO GUARD SCRIPT PRETENDS OTHERWISE. Observing this
needs a built engine, a nested compositor and a key injected into it, and
nothing in this repo can inject one -- `under-wayland.sh` runs sway's
headless backend with `WLR_LIBINPUT_NO_DEVICES=1`, so there is no
keyboard to press. What a guard would have to be is `WAYLAND_DEBUG=1` and
a grep for `inhibit_shortcuts` on the wire; `ROADMAP.md` carries it as
open work rather than a check that proves nothing.

WRITTEN BY HAND, AGAINST THE PIN RATHER THAN IN A CHECKOUT, with both
blobs computed from the pinned files rather than zeroed: no other patch
in the series touches either file, so each pre-image is the pin's and
each post-image is the pin's file with these lines in it.
---
 .../wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.cc   | 82 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.h    | 13 +++
 2 files changed, 95 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.cc b/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.cc
index 8fbac8ac7d..85a080b51a 100644
--- a/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.cc
+++ b/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.cc
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 
 #include <string>
 
+#include "base/command_line.h"
 #include "base/logging.h"
 #include "base/nix/xdg_util.h"
 #include "base/numerics/safe_conversions.h"
@@ -28,9 +29,11 @@
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_data_drag_controller.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_event_source.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_frame_manager.h"
+#include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_keyboard.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_output.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_output_manager.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_popup.h"
+#include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_seat.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_window.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_window_drag_controller.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_zwp_pointer_constraints.h"
@@ -46,6 +49,18 @@ namespace ui {
 
 namespace {
 
+// Domicile: the launcher passes this when, and only when, this engine is a
+// window inside somebody else's Wayland session; see
+// packages/domicile-launch/src/spawn.rs for why the decision is made out there
+// rather than inferred from the ozone platform in here.
+//
+// Spelled again rather than shared with the fork's other switches in
+// components/domicile/common/domicile_scheme.h: //components sits on top of
+// //ui, so an ozone platform that included that header would invert the
+// dependency. One more string literal is the cheaper of the two prices.
+constexpr char kInhibitHostShortcutsSwitch[] =
+    "domicile-inhibit-host-shortcuts";
+
 bool ShouldSetBounds(PlatformWindowState state) {
   return state == PlatformWindowState::kNormal;
 }
@@ -189,6 +204,9 @@ void WaylandToplevelWindow::Hide() {
   xdg_toplevel_.reset();
   toplevel_session_.reset();
   appmenu_.reset();
+  // Domicile: an unmapped window holds nobody's keyboard, and the next Show()
+  // makes a new one. See InhibitHostKeyboardShortcuts().
+  shortcuts_inhibitor_.reset();
 
   WaylandWindow::Hide();
   ClearInFlightRequestsSerial();
@@ -1029,6 +1047,70 @@ void WaylandToplevelWindow::SetUpShellIntegration() {
   // This method should be called after the XDG surface is initialized.
   DCHECK(xdg_toplevel_);
   TryAnnounceAppmenu();
+  InhibitHostKeyboardShortcuts();
+}
+
+// Domicile: a desktop nested in somebody else's Wayland session has no Meta
+// key, and nothing anywhere says so.
+//
+// Every binding the shell claims is a Meta chord -- see
+// packages/shell-manganese/src/keyboard/bindings.ts -- and it claims them
+// through the fork's own shortcut registry, which reaches no further than this
+// browser process. A host compositor matches its bindings BEFORE it sends a key
+// to the focused client, and does not forward what it took, so under sway
+// Mod4+Return opened sway's terminal and this engine was never told a key had
+// been pressed at all. Nothing failed, nothing logged, and the shell looked
+// broken.
+//
+// zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibit_unstable_v1 is the protocol for saying "not
+// while this surface has the keyboard", and the compositor scopes it exactly
+// that way: sway activates the inhibitor when the surface takes keyboard focus
+// and deactivates it when focus leaves, so a user's bindings come back the
+// moment they click away. What they have while the desktop IS focused is
+// `bindsym --inhibited`, and docs/RUNNING-A-DESKTOP.md says so -- a keymap that
+// goes dead with no way out is a worse desktop than one with no Meta key.
+//
+// UPSTREAM HAS THE CALL AND NO PATH TO IT. WaylandConnection already binds
+// zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibit_manager_v1, and
+// WaylandKeyboard::CreateShortcutsInhibitor is upstream's own request against
+// it -- which is what this drives, rather than issuing inhibit_shortcuts a
+// second time from here. Its only caller is
+// WaylandKeyboard::CreateKeyboardHook, reached from
+// OzonePlatformWayland::CreateKeyboardHook, which is the Keyboard Lock API: a
+// page calling navigator.keyboard.lock() on a window it asked to make
+// fullscreen. A nested desktop is deliberately not fullscreen --
+// --start-fullscreen belongs to the drm platform, see spawn.rs -- and putting
+// the lock call in the shell would make every shell responsible for a property
+// of a window it does not own. The desktop is not asking to lock keys for a
+// page. It is saying that it is the desktop.
+//
+// (WaylandSurface::SetKeyboardShortcutsInhibition(bool) reads like the seam for
+// this and is not one: at fa0ce55e it is declared in wayland_surface.h and
+// never defined or called anywhere in the tree.)
+void WaylandToplevelWindow::InhibitHostKeyboardShortcuts() {
+  if (!base::CommandLine::ForCurrentProcess()->HasSwitch(
+          kInhibitHostShortcutsSwitch)) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  // LOUDLY on both arms, and neither is a bug in here: a seat that announced no
+  // keyboard, or a host compositor that does not carry the protocol. The
+  // symptom of either is a desktop where every Meta chord does something else,
+  // which reads as the shell being broken, so the log line is what turns an
+  // afternoon into a sentence.
+  auto* seat = connection()->seat();
+  if (!seat || !seat->keyboard()) {
+    LOG(ERROR) << "domicile: no keyboard on the seat, so the host compositor "
+                  "keeps its shortcuts and the shell's Meta bindings will not "
+                  "arrive";
+    return;
+  }
+  shortcuts_inhibitor_ = seat->keyboard()->CreateShortcutsInhibitor(this);
+  if (!shortcuts_inhibitor_) {
+    LOG(ERROR) << "domicile: the host compositor offers no "
+                  "zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibit_manager_v1, so it keeps its "
+                  "shortcuts and the shell's Meta bindings will not arrive";
+  }
 }
 
 void WaylandToplevelWindow::OnDecorationModeChanged() {
diff --git a/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.h b/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.h
index 15cc9a2d4a..060ff00f7c 100644
--- a/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.h
+++ b/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_toplevel_window.h
@@ -209,6 +209,11 @@ class WaylandToplevelWindow : public WaylandWindow,
   // available.
   void SetUpShellIntegration();
 
+  // Domicile: asks the host compositor to stop matching its own keyboard
+  // shortcuts while this window has the keyboard. The definition carries the
+  // whole argument, including why it is a command-line switch.
+  void InhibitHostKeyboardShortcuts();
+
   // Sets decoration mode for a window.
   void OnDecorationModeChanged();
 
@@ -281,6 +286,14 @@ class WaylandToplevelWindow : public WaylandWindow,
   std::string appmenu_service_name_;
   std::string appmenu_object_path_;
 
+  // Domicile: held for exactly as long as this window is mapped. Destroying it
+  // is what hands the host compositor its shortcuts back, so it is reset in
+  // Hide() beside appmenu_ above -- Show() builds a fresh xdg_toplevel and runs
+  // SetUpShellIntegration() again, and a second inhibitor for a surface and
+  // seat that already have one is a protocol error the compositor answers by
+  // dropping the connection.
+  wl::Object<zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibitor_v1> shortcuts_inhibitor_;
+
   base::WeakPtrFactory<WaylandToplevelWindow> weak_ptr_factory_{this};
 };
 
